doc0:Reading Post The Reading Post (until 2009, the Reading Evening Post) was an English local newspaper covering Reading, Berkshire and surrounding areas. The title page of the paper featured the Maiwand Lion, a famous local landmark at Forbury Gardens. The paper was most recently published by Surrey & Berkshire Media Ltd., a division of Trinity Mirror plc. In 2009, the paper changed from daily publication to publishing weekly on a Wednesday as a paid-for paper with a free edition on a Friday titled ""Get Reading"". The paper was previously promoted as an evening paper and published Monday to Friday. In
doc1:Maiwand Lion The Maiwand Lion is a sculpture and war memorial in the Forbury Gardens, a public park in the town of Reading, in the English county of Berkshire. The statue was named after the Battle of Maiwand and was erected in 1884 to commemorate the deaths of 329 men from the 66th (Berkshire) Regiment of Foot during the campaign in the Second Anglo-Afghan War in Afghanistan between 1878 and 1880. It is sometimes known locally as the Forbury Lion. The inscription on the plinth reads as follows: This monument records the names and commemorates the valour and devotion of
doc2:recent years, all editions were tabloid though it was launched as a broadsheet. In February 2010 the division of Guardian Media Group that included the ""Reading Evening Post"" was sold to Trinity Mirror. This sale included 22 titles across the north of England and in Surrey and Berkshire. The ""Reading Evening Post"" was named Regional Newspaper of the Year for the second year running at the 2004 Newspaper Awards. The final issue was published on 17 December 2014. Reading Post The Reading Post (until 2009, the Reading Evening Post) was an English local newspaper covering Reading, Berkshire and surrounding areas.
