doc0:Cocking Foundry Cocking Foundry (also known as Chorley Iron Foundry) is an abandoned iron foundry in the South Downs of England. It was situated to the north of the village of Cocking, West Sussex and was active for most of the 19th century. The foundry's output included wheels for watermills, some of which remain in use. The foundry was situated at on Costers Brook, a northward flowing tributary of the River Rother, about north of Cocking and south of Midhurst. The site is now on private property and few traces remain visible. The earliest known reference to the foundry at
